================ ADVERSARIAL ADDENDUM (kps-Sx-wf, exact) ================
FAST HUNT (4000+ structured + 6000 random primitive true-wide k-sets, MAXE<=80):
  k  #wide  best_p0   cap      QB      >cap  >QB
  8   807   0.20218  0.38146  0.19660  NO   YES (witness [0,19,20,21,22,23,24,25])
  9   787   0.31812  0.49426  0.36210  NO   NO
 10   794   0.42690  0.60440  0.44789  NO   NO
 11   776   0.50759  0.72527  0.53125  NO   NO
 12   785   0.58149  0.85714  0.60224  NO   NO
  => 0 violations of p0<=cap_k.  Core conclusion SURVIVES the hunt.
  => 2 rows EXCEED the claimed wide-bound QB(k-1) (the p0_inf worst case).

PROBE of the over-QB witness (EXACT):
  [0,19,20,21,22,23,24,25]: p0 = 9524621/47108600 = 0.2021843 > QB(7)=0.19660,
     margin to cap_8 = 0.17928 (safe).
  TRUE finite-scale max of {0} U (g+consec_7): p0 = 0.2170306 at g=9
     => EXCEEDS QB by +0.02043 ; still 0.16443 under cap_8.
  consec_7 U {g} (far singleton): max 0.32721 at g=7 (=consec_8, span-14 case).

INTERPRETATION (the surviving gap, made precise):
  p0_inf (the decorrelated LIMIT) is NOT an upper bound for the actual finite-
  scale p0(E).  Finite-scale p0 sits ABOVE p0_inf by ERR up to ~0.020 in the
  wide region.  The claim's part-(B) statement "worst p0_inf = QB < cap" is
  TRUE but bounds only the limit, not p0(E).  The theorem p0(E)<=cap_k still
  holds on all sampled rows because margin (>=0.132) >> observed ERR (<=0.020),
  but CLOSING it rigorously REQUIRES the deferred error-aggregation inequality
  p0(E) <= p0_inf + sum_far(6/49)V_i/g_i, which is NOT proved closed-form.
  This is exactly the SOLE analytic gap the claim itself names.  VERDICT: the
  reduction is sound and the bottom line is well-supported, but it is a
  REDUCTION-to-conjecture, NOT a completed proof.
